          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 11 — Keeping Time with the Skies"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.922,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "181"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.087,
            0.395,
            0.108
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Our scientific heritage"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.12,
            0.821,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For thousands of years, people—including those in India—have been observing the sky and developing calendars. People in ancient times did not know that Earth revolves around the Sun, and lacked modern instruments. Yet through years of careful sky observations, they noticed patterns and cycles"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.188,
            0.465,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "in natural events. Hence, they could determine that the length of the year was approximately 365 days allowing them to create calendars."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.255,
            0.466,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For example, careful observation reveals that the Sun does not always rise exactly in the East. In summer, it rises a little northward of East and in winter a little southward of East. These extremes happen on the solstices, around June 21 and December 21 each year. The Sun’s"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.485,
            0.192,
            0.64,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sun rises exactly in the East and sets exactly in the west on equinoxes"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.703,
            0.192,
            0.801,
            0.219
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Path of Sun on June solstice"
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.47,
            0.236,
            0.557,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Path of Sun on December solstice"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.471,
            0.223,
            0.819,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 10,
              "bbox": [
                0.471,
                0.223,
                0.819,
                0.373
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_10.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.563,
                0.373,
                0.781,
                0.387
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Uttarayan from December to June"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.39,
            0.82,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "apparent northward movement from December to June is called Uttarayan, and its apparent southward movement from June to December is Dakshinayan. This cycle repeats every year and is closely linked to the changing seasons. The Taittirīya Saṁhitā records it in the verse 6.5.3:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.46,
            0.632,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "तस्‍‍माादाादि त्‍‍य: षण्माासाा दक्षि�णेेनैैति षडुुत्तरेेण ।"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.48,
            0.82,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "“Thus the Sun moves southwards for six months and northwards for six months.”"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.513,
            0.821,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the past, the equinoxes and solstices were also tracked by identifying the stars that rose at sunset. Ancient Indian texts like the Surya Siddhanta noted that the pattern of stars, Capricorn (called Makar in India), would be in the background of the Sun around the winter solstice during those ancient times."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.311,
            0.585,
            0.634,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "भाानोोर्ममकरसंस्ं ‍ङ्् ्क्राान्तेःः� षण्माासाा उत्तराायणम्् ।",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.29,
            0.609,
            0.656,
            0.627
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "कर्क्‍का ादेेस्तुु तथैैव स्याात््षण्माासाा दक्षि�णाायनम्् ।।9।।"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.627,
            0.82,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Translation: From the moment of the Sun’s entrance into the constellation of Capricorn, six months make up its northward progress (Uttarayana), so likewise from the moment of entrance into the constellation of Cancer, six months are its southward progress (Dakshinayana)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.694,
            0.82,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Over the years, different types of calendars have evolved based on specific needs. A number of these calendars are used in different parts of India to track time and celebrating festivals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.826,
            0.586,
            0.939,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 20,
              "bbox": [
                0.826,
                0.586,
                0.939,
                0.758
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_11_image_20.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.781,
            0.422,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "11.2.3 Luni-solar calendars"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.802,
            0.743,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There is another kind of calendar which primarily uses the Moon’s phases for counting days and months but also make adjustments to stay in sync with the cycle of seasons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.859,
            0.744,
            0.898
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The 12 lunar months add up to 354 days and thus fall short by nearly 11 days compared to the solar year. Thus every 2–3 years,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
